WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.420
Hand wash and dish wash by percentage formula.

2
00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:08.640
This is the video for Module 2 of Liquid Soap, Shampoo and Disinfectants.

3
00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:16.240
In this module you move from recipes to real formulas, written in percentages of the total weight.

4
00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:25.340
That way the same sheet works for a 2 kg test or a 200 kg batch. You only multiply by the batch weight.

5
00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:30.500
You will choose a dish liquid by testing how many plates it washes.

6
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:37.680
And you will learn to control thickness, colour and clarity, because that is what a customer sees first.

7
00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:50.980
The starting hand wash uses SLES to clean and foam, CAPB to make it milder, and glycerin so hands feel less dry. Its active matter is 9.9%, a moderate level.

8
00:00:52.230 --> 00:01:08.640
For example, Chiamaka in Enugu makes 10 kg. She multiplies each percentage by 10,000 grams and divides by 100. SLES at 12% is 1,200 grams. CAPB at 5% is 500 grams.

9
00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:13.600
Glycerin is 200 grams, and salt 100 grams.

10
00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:22.060
Preservative is 80 grams, fragrance 40, EDTA 10, and water 7,870 grams.

11
00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:31.910
She checks that it all adds up to 10,000 grams, and ticks each line as she adds it. To double the batch later, she simply doubles each line.

12
00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.170
Weigh the water, keep back about 10%, and dissolve the EDTA.

13
00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.790
Add the SLES paste in pieces, stirring slowly so you do not whip in air.

14
00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.560
When it is smooth, add CAPB and glycerin.

15
00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:53.310
Premix the fragrance with a little CAPB to reduce cloudiness, then add preservative and colour.

16
00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:04.030
Rest 15 minutes, adjust the pH, and only then add salt, in small portions, so you can thicken accurately. Rest overnight before filling.

17
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:14.310
Wear gloves and goggles. And do not label a hand wash 'antibacterial' unless the claim is tested and approved for your registered product.

18
00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:31.220
A dish liquid is judged on one thing: how many plates one squeeze washes before the foam dies. So test it. Use identical oily plates, the same 5 litres of warm water and the same dose. Wash until the foam disappears, and count.

19
00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:44.730
Formula A costs about 1,400 naira per kg to make, so a dose costs about 7 naira. It washed 18 plates. That is about 0.39 naira per plate.

20
00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:57.670
Formula B is cheaper, about 1,050 naira per kg, so 5.25 naira a dose. But it washed only 12 plates, about 0.44 naira per plate.

21
00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:06.170
Formula A costs more per litre but less per plate. It also beat the market brand, which washed 15. So Zainab chooses A.

22
00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:20.570
Hands soak in dish water for a long time, so keep the pH around 6 to 8. Never add caustic soda or soda ash for extra power. It irritates skin and can dull aluminium pots.

23
00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:36.250
Never pack dish liquid in used soft-drink or water bottles, because someone may drink it by mistake. Use proper bottles with 'Keep out of reach of children' and first aid instructions such as 'If swallowed, rinse mouth and seek medical advice.'

24
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:47.300
Measure thickness with a flow test. Fill a 60 ml syringe to the 50 ml mark, release, and time how long it takes to drain to 10 ml.

25
00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:56.330
For example, Ruth in Jos takes four 200 g samples of her 20 kg batch, and adds salt at four levels.

26
00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:03.560
The flow times are 4, 9, 21 and 14 seconds. Thickness rose, then fell.

27
00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:10.890
So the peak is around 1.5%, which fits her target of 15 to 25 seconds.

28
00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:21.180
For 20 kg she needs 300 grams of salt. She dissolves it as a brine and adds it in three portions, testing after each.

29
00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:30.820
Salt thickens only up to a peak. Past it, the product thins, and you cannot take salt out. So always test small samples first.

30
00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:43.030
So remember. Work in percentages. Add salt last. Choose your dish liquid by cost per plate. Dose colour from a 1% stock solution so it is easy to weigh.

31
00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:52.490
Check every batch against a written specification and a standard sample. A batch that fails is held back, never sold because it is almost fine.

32
00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:58.880
Now make a 2 kg test batch, run your own plate test, and draw your salt curve.

33
00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:01.170
Continue with the lessons below.
